--- b9 medium.en: But a waiver has two gates, and the first one is fault. The agency can find you at fault only with evidence that you gave information you knew was wrong, failed to give information you knew mattered, or accepted a payment you knew was incorrect. Without that evidence, you are not at fault. The manual also presumes you are not at fault if you reported a change on time, if payments kept coming after you reported it, or if the agency itself made an error in computing your benefit.
